<div>
<div class="episode"><b>04</b><span>NBA TIME MACHINE / AN ORIGINAL INVESTIGATION</span></div>
<h1 id="article-title">We tried to find the next Larry Bird. <em>The model picked Michael Porter Jr.</em></h1>
<p class="dek">A 1985 MVP season, 279 modern players, five surprisingly familiar forwards and one question statistics alone cannot settle: what actually made Larry Bird <em>Larry Bird</em>?</p>
<div class="byline"><span>By <strong>{author}</strong></span><span>November 1, 2026</span><span>Research / 12 min read</span></div>
</div>
<figure class="hero-photo">
<span class="photo-index">BOSTON / NO. 33</span>
<img src={heroImage} alt="Larry Bird in his Boston Celtics uniform during his playing career" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
<figcaption>Larry Bird. Photograph sourced from <a href="https://www.nba.com/news/history-nba-legend-larry-bird" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NBA.com</a>; image rights remain with the original photographer / NBA.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section class="stats-strip" aria-label="Larry Bird's 1984–85 regular-season numbers">
<div><strong>28.7</strong><small>Points per game</small></div>
<div><strong>10.5</strong><small>Rebounds per game</small></div>
<div><strong>6.6</strong><small>Assists per game</small></div>
<div><strong>42.7%</strong><small>Three-point accuracy</small></div>
<div><strong>63–19</strong><small>Boston's record</small></div>
</section>
<div class="story-grid">
<article class="article" aria-label="Article">
<p class="lead">The computer did not choose the player most people would call the next Larry Bird. It chose Michael Porter Jr., a 6-foot-10 shooter who spent much of his career making defenders pay for giving him an inch of space.</p>
<p>That result sounds absurd if your picture of Bird is a passing savant, a three-time MVP, an orchestrator who could read a possession before anyone else recognized what was happening. Porter is not that player. Yet the more we examined the model's answer, the harder it became to dismiss. The shooting threat, the height, the scoring workload and the forward-sized frame all leave recognizable traces.</p>
<p>Then came the runner-up: Jalen Johnson. He looks less like Bird as a shooter but more familiar as a tall forward who rebounds, handles, sees the floor and turns one possession into the next. One candidate gives us a version of Bird's scoring geometry. Another gives us something of his all-court involvement.</p>
<p><strong>Neither gives us the full picture.</strong> And that is where this investigation begins.</p>
<h2><span class="section-index">01 / BEFORE THE ALGORITHM</span>Who was Larry Bird, really?</h2>
<p>Larry Joe Bird came from southern Indiana, where basketball was less a pastime than a local language. From French Lick and Springs Valley High School, he went to Indiana State, carrying a small school's program to the 1979 NCAA championship game. On the other side of that final was Magic Johnson and Michigan State. Their rivalry did not begin in the NBA, but it would help define the NBA's next decade.</p>
<p>Boston drafted Bird in 1978, and he arrived in 1979 at a moment when the Celtics badly needed a new centre of gravity. By the time his career ended, he had won three championships, three consecutive MVP awards from 1984 through 1986, two Finals MVPs and twelve All-Star selections. Alongside Magic's Lakers, Bird's Celtics helped turn the league's greatest rivalry into appointment viewing.</p>
<p>But the résumé is the least interesting way to explain him. Bird was a 6-foot-9 forward with the vision of an elite point guard, the rebounding instincts of a bigger player and the release of a specialist shooter. He could score without monopolizing the ball, pass before the opening looked open and exploit a defender's smallest positional mistake. He didn't require spectacular speed or an enormous vertical leap to control a game. His edges were anticipation, timing, balance, touch, footwork and an almost stubborn insistence that a correct play be made.</p>
<p>Much of the mythology is about confidence and trash talk. The basketball reality is more useful. Bird was constantly <strong>creating decisions</strong> for the defense: help on his catch and a cutter might be open; stay home and he might shoot; crowd his jumper and he could find the back door; send a small defender and he could work in the post. His passing was not a decorative addition to his scoring. It made his scoring harder to contain.</p>
<blockquote>Bird wasn't only a shooter who could pass. He was a forward who could determine what the defense was allowed to do.</blockquote>
<p>His 1984–85 season makes the point. Boston went 63–19 and reached the NBA Finals, where the Lakers won in six games. Bird won his second straight MVP by averaging 28.7 points, 10.5 rebounds and 6.6 assists, shooting 52.2% from the field, 42.7% from three and 88.2% at the line. He played nearly forty minutes a night and still finished as one of the league's most complete offensive players.</p>
<p>On March 12, 1985, he scored 60 points against Atlanta on 22-for-36 shooting. The performance matters beyond its total: a forward who already carried Boston's passing and rebounding responsibilities could also have a transcendent individual scoring night. There was no tidy division between creator, finisher and organizer.</p>
<p>One historical detail is easy to miss when looking at that 42.7% three-point figure: Bird attempted only about <strong>1.6 threes per game</strong> that season. The shot was a weapon, but this was not a modern high-volume three-point offense. Reading his shooting percentage as if it came from today's spacing and shot diet would be an error.</p>
<h2><span class="section-index">02 / THE QUESTION</span>Can a modern player reproduce the shape of that season?</h2>
<p>We took Bird's <strong>1984–85 regular season</strong>, not his career averages or a hand-picked mixture of his best seasons, and compared it against the Anticipate dataset of 279 NBA players from 2025–26. Each modern player cleared 1,000 regular-season minutes; traded players were counted once with combined season totals.</p>
<p>We scored seven features: points per 100 possessions, true shooting relative to that player's league, assist percentage, total rebound percentage, usage percentage, steal percentage and block percentage. A second component measures listed height and weight. A third applies a penalty when a player's broad position differs from Bird's.</p>
<p>The default weighting is <strong>70% statistical profile, 20% physical measurements and 10% positional group</strong>. We standardize differences against the same modern-player population and calculate a combined distance. A smaller number means a closer profile. It does <em>not</em> mean a player is a certain percentage as good as Bird.</p>
<p>The historical inputs are now checked against the published 1984–85 season: 34.3 points per 100 possessions, 25.7% assist share, 14.7% total rebound rate and 28.5% usage. Bird's 58.5% true shooting was 4.2 percentage points above the NBA's 54.3% average. He is listed at 206 centimetres and 220 pounds.</p>
<p>Three-point percentage is retained for editorial analysis but intentionally left out of the first model. That choice prevents Bird's low-attempt 1985 accuracy from being treated as equivalent to a modern high-volume perimeter role. It also gives the model a blind spot we need to discuss openly.</p>
</article>
