<div class="story-grid">
<article class="article">
<h2><span class="section-index">04 / THE SURPRISING WINNER</span>Why Michael Porter Jr. makes sense</h2>
<p>The first thing to understand is that our model is not saying Porter plays the same role Bird did. It is saying their measurable profiles, taken together, are closer than those of the other qualifying players. That distinction is essential.</p>
<p>Bird's listed height and weight were 206 centimetres and 220 pounds. The Anticipate modern data gives Porter 208 centimetres and 218 pounds. Their bodies are remarkably close by those limited measurements. Both are tall forwards capable of scoring over the top of a contest, and both make a defender think twice about surrendering an uncontested look.</p>
<p>The scoring workload matters too. Bird produced 34.3 points per 100 possessions in 1984–85. Porter produced 36.9 per 100 in 2025–26, while carrying a substantially larger scoring role for Brooklyn than the finishing-focused version seen in his Denver years. In his 52 Brooklyn games, Porter averaged 24.2 points, 7.1 rebounds and 3.0 assists. It is not an identical stat line, but the high-volume scoring threat and forward-sized body help explain the model's result.</p>
<p>There is also a basketball explanation beyond body measurements. Porter can punish defenders without needing to pound the dribble. His shooting touch, reach and ability to catch and rise can bend a defense by forcing a bigger, more urgent closeout. Bird also weaponized the threat of a jump shot to make the next action available. That is a meaningful resemblance in the <em>way a defense responds</em>, even if their preferred shots, movement patterns and passing decisions differ.</p>
<p>Porter's accuracy from three in 2025–26 was 36.3%, on 9.3 attempts per game in Brooklyn; Bird hit 42.7%, on just 1.6 attempts per game in 1984–85. That does not prove either player was the superior era-adjusted shooter. It shows a major difference in the job their teams asked them to perform. We cannot pretend the same raw percentage describes the same offensive environment.</p>
<h3>Where the comparison breaks</h3>
<p>Bird registered a 25.7% assist rate and 6.6 assists per game during his MVP campaign. Porter had an 18.2% assist rate and 3.0 assists per game in his 2025–26 Brooklyn season. Bird's involvement was broader, and his passing was not simply a volume stat. He used a threat to score to manufacture passing windows, often acting as the organizer of a possession. Porter can move the ball and make good reads, but his most recognizable value remains shotmaking and the attention it demands.</p>
<p>On the glass, Bird's 14.7% rebound rate also exceeds Porter's 12.6%. The model notices those differences; it just decides the combined scoring and physical fit outweighs them. Porter's No. 1 position becomes reasonable once you understand the rules. It does not settle the basketball argument.</p>
<blockquote>Porter is a plausible answer to “Who has Bird's scoring silhouette?” He is a much less complete answer to “Who plays Bird's game?”</blockquote>
<h2><span class="section-index">05 / THE BEST COUNTERARGUMENT</span>Jalen Johnson offers a different kind of Bird resemblance</h2>
<p>If Porter is the shooter-shaped answer, Johnson is the connective answer.</p>
<p>In 2025–26, Johnson averaged <strong>22.5 points, 10.3 rebounds and 7.9 assists</strong> for Atlanta. Those are conspicuously close to Bird's rebounding total and comfortably above his per-game assist number, although differences in pace, role and teammates prevent a direct one-to-one conclusion. He is a tall forward who can secure the ball, accelerate into transition, find teammates ahead of the defense and attack from the same possession he just finished on the glass.</p>
<p>The rate statistics reveal why the counterargument has force. Johnson posted a 15.9% total rebound percentage and a 33.5% assist percentage. Bird's corresponding values were 14.7% and 25.7%. Johnson's creation responsibilities and rebounding involvement are not side notes; they are major parts of his statistical identity. That feels familiar to anyone who remembers Bird making plays from everywhere on the floor.</p>
<p>Beyond the numbers, Johnson can connect possessions. He does not have to be parked in the corner waiting to finish a pass. He can turn a rebound into a full-speed attack, initiate an offense from the frontcourt and create an advantage before the defense resets. Bird did not play like a modern transition slasher, but he did make the entire team better by seeing those windows early and keeping the ball moving. The similarity is in their <em>all-court influence</em>, not in identical technique.</p>
<p>But Johnson is not Bird as a shooter. His 2025–26 three-point percentage was 35.2%, and the broader shape of his scoring comes from a modern athletic forward's driving and passing game. Bird's catch-to-release speed, post footwork, midrange touch and ability to punish a small defensive lapse are not reproduced simply by pairing ten rebounds with eight assists.</p>
<p>So why doesn't Johnson win? In the published model, his 30.2 points per 100 possessions sit further from Bird's 34.3 than Porter's 36.9 do. He is slightly farther away in height, and the statistical and physical components together put him second by a small margin: <strong>0.4996 against Porter's 0.4853</strong>. The difference is real in this formula, but it is not an enormous separation.</p>
<div class="scene" aria-label="Two ways to resemble Larry Bird">
<div class="scene-card"><span class="micro">THE PORTER CASE</span><h3>Scoring geometry</h3><p>Height, scoring volume, catch-and-shoot threat, quick decisions against aggressive closeouts. Weakest overlap: Bird's passing and rebounding influence.</p></div>
<div class="scene-card"><span class="micro">THE JOHNSON CASE</span><h3>All-court connection</h3><p>Rebounding, frontcourt initiation, the rebound-to-pass chain and creation for others. Weakest overlap: Bird's shooting style and touch.</p></div>
</div>
<h2><span class="section-index">06 / THE OTHER THREE</span>What Wagner, Avdija and LeBron tell us</h2>
<p><strong>Franz Wagner, third.</strong> At 208 centimetres and 220 pounds in the model's data, Wagner almost duplicates Bird's listed build. His forward scoring and ability to operate with and without the ball make him an understandable entry. But his 18.2% assist rate and 9.7% rebound rate sit well below Bird's historical marks. The physical match is not the complete player.</p>
<p><strong>Deni Avdija, fourth.</strong> Avdija's 34.7 points per 100 possessions and 32.2% assist percentage offer a particularly interesting combination of scoring volume and creation. He is a big forward who can handle, attack and produce passing opportunities. His modern driving pressure differs from Bird's slower, more deliberate manipulation of defenders, but the connection between scoring and playmaking is worth seeing.</p>
<p><strong>LeBron James, fifth.</strong> The famous name may be the easiest stylistic argument to make at a glance: a large forward who sees the court, controls the ball and can dictate an offense. The model gives LeBron a comparatively strong statistical match but penalizes a bigger listed weight difference, 250 pounds versus Bird's 220. More importantly, even an excellent model cannot equate their passing styles, on-ball responsibilities or historical influence with one distance score.</p>
<p>In a different weighting system, their order changes. That leads us to the second experiment.</p>
</article>
