<section class="full" id="methodology">
<span class="section-kicker">RESEARCH NOTES / METHODS / SOURCES</span>
<h2 class="section-title">How we built this investigation</h2>
<div class="scene">
<div class="scene-card"><span class="micro">01 / THE POPULATION</span><h3>One season versus 279</h3><p>Bird's 1984–85 regular season against all 279 qualified 2025–26 players in the Anticipate dataset (1,000+ minutes each, no duplicate traded-player rows).</p></div>
<div class="scene-card"><span class="micro">02 / THE FEATURES</span><h3>Production, body and role</h3><p>Points per 100, era-relative TS, AST%, TRB%, USG%, STL%, BLK%; height and weight; broad positional class. Three-point percentage is context only.</p></div>
<div class="scene-card"><span class="micro">03 / THE CALCULATION</span><h3>Standardized distance</h3><p>Each numerical difference is divided by the modern eligible group's standard deviation, squared and averaged within its component, then combined using the stated weights.</p></div>
<div class="scene-card"><span class="micro">04 / THE CHECKS</span><h3>Independent recomputation</h3><p>We verified Bird's historical reference metrics against published season records and reproduced the full-population top five and two alternative weightings from the repository data.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="mathbox" aria-label="Published model distance formula">distance = sqrt(0.70 × statistical_distance² + 0.20 × physical_distance² + 0.10 × position_penalty)</div>
<p class="method-note" style="margin-top:17px">Statistical distance is the average squared standardized difference across seven metrics; physical distance averages standardized height and weight differences. Position penalty is 0 for matching forward/guard/center group, 1 otherwise. The model deliberately does not claim stylistic equivalence or comparable overall greatness.</p>
<h3 style="font-size:22px;margin:36px 0 12px">Limitations that matter</h3>
<div class="note"><strong>Use this as a comparison tool, not a verdict machine.</strong> These are rounded, season-specific inputs; listed body measurements may not perfectly represent playing size. Historical and modern roles differ. Percentages capture outcomes, not play types. Seven features cannot measure shot-making difficulty, lineup context, defensive versatility or the exact quality of a read. Model weights are editorial. The source code and original inputs are public so readers can audit or modify the assumptions.</div>
